INT. CENTRAL TELEPHONE EXCHANGE - SWITCHBOARD ROOM - NIGHT, 1924

Rows of operators in shirtwaists sit at a wall of brass jacks. Cords hang like ivy. A wall clock ticks.

EUNICE PRUITT (20s, headset, pencil in her hair) works her board with quick wrists. Behind her, MISS LAVINIA CROFT (50s, supervisor, high collar, spectacles on a ribbon) watches over her shoulder.

                    EUNICE
          Number, please.

                    HARCOURT (V.O.)
                (phone, crackling)
          Operator, I want Elmwood two-two-one.

                    EUNICE
          One moment, sir.

                    MISS CROFT
          Plug it in.

                    EUNICE
          I am.

                    HARCOURT (V.O.)
                (phone)
          Hello? Hello?

                    MISS CROFT
          Pinch it, Miss Pruitt, don't push.

                    EUNICE
          I am pinching it.

                    HARCOURT (V.O.)
                (phone)
          I have been waiting two minutes!

                    EUNICE
          Sir, I assure you, it's two seconds.

                    MISS CROFT
          Do not argue with a customer.

                    EUNICE
          He is not a customer, Miss Croft, he is a bellow.

                    HARCOURT (V.O.)
                (phone)
          What did you say?

                    EUNICE
          Elmwood two-two-one, sir. Connecting now.

She jabs the cord home. A lamp flickers green. Miss Croft nods, a barely perceptible quiver of approval.

                    MISS CROFT
          Better.
